general elections are supposed to happen  every five years but this is the third  one since 2015 so why is it happening  when Theresa May became prime minister  after David Cameron stepped down she  promised her government would deliver  brexit but with a slender majority of  only 12 in the House of Commons she went  back to the country in 2017 to win more  seats for the Conservatives and bolster  her brexit negotiating position strong  and stable strong and stable strong and  stable  she made a gamble and it backfired  spectacularly instead of strengthening  her position she lost her majority her  brexit plans were voted down three times  she faced repeated calls to resign from  within her own party and by the summer  of 2019 it was clear she could no longer  lead it's been the honor of my life to  have had the opportunity to serve the  country I love  Boris Johnson won the contest to replace  her he tried to suspend Parliament for  five weeks limiting the amount of time  MPs would have to scrutinize his brexit  strategy but the highest court in the  land said this was unlawful as it  prevented debate around his policy  strongly disagree with what the justices  have found I don't think that it's right  meanwhile 21 conservative MPs rebelled  against the prime minister voting  against him to stop the threat of a No  Deal brexit they were suspended from the  party  [Applause]  Boris Johnson was now well short of a  parliamentary majority he could have  tried to govern as Prime Minister until  2022 but instead he's called for an  early general election hoping to  reshuffle the arithmetic in Parliament  and win more conservative MPs that will  back his brexit plan labour the Liberal  Democrats and the Scottish National  Party signed up for the election but  only once the immediate threat of a No  Deal brexit was off the table so finally  the December election was agreed and the  country must vote for its third  Parliament in five years  [Music]
 

